<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST4509"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Dpinva<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Dpinva<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, January 22, 2005 - 7:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><b><i>Retraction: &#34;No DNA match means no conviction&#34; from above post. Of course he could still be convicted by other means. I&#39;m talking about BTK, not the December guy; December Guy&#39;s DNA non-match satisfies the police, and satisifies me. </i></b> <BR> <BR>Having a DNA test that shows a clearly negative result goes a LONG way towards establishing reasonable doubt. No prosecutor in thier right mind would indict someone who has been DNA excluded and try to make a soley circumstantial case. It just wouldn&#39;t happen. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
